A function can perform actions like printing to the console, but it can also send a value back to the code that called it using the return keyword.

When you use console.log inside a function, the value appears in the console, but the function itself does not provide a result to the caller. If you do not include a return statement, the function finishes and gives back undefined by default.

Consider this example:

function calculateSum(a, b) {
  return a + b;
}

let result = calculateSum(5, 10);

In this code, the function calculateSum takes two arguments, adds them, and returns the total. The variable result then stores the value 15. Because the function uses return, the caller can capture and use that value later. If you only used console.log inside the function, the variable result would be undefined because nothing was sent back.

