An if statement allows your code to make decisions. It checks a condition, which is a comparison that results in true or false. If the condition is true, the code inside the if block runs. If the condition is false, the code inside the else block runs instead.

Use === to check if two values are exactly the same. Use = to assign a value to a variable. These perform different tasks.

Here is an example:

const score = 50;
if (score === 100) {
  console.log("Perfect score");
} else {
  console.log("Keep trying");
}

In this code, the variable score is compared to 100 using strict equality. Since 50 is not equal to 100, the condition is false. Therefore, the program skips the first block and executes the code inside the else block, printing "Keep trying" to the console.

